# `SELECT ... FROM` after an operator: a rewrite would drop `where`, so only the help is given.
mode: syntax
---- query
from t | where x > 1 | SELECT a FROM t
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..39
  PIPELINE@0..38
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "t"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    WHERE_OP@9..20
      IDENT@9..14 "where"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      BIN_EXPR@15..20
        NAME_REF@15..16
          IDENT@15..16 "x"
        WHITESPACE@16..17 " "
        GT@17..18 ">"
        WHITESPACE@18..19 " "
        LITERAL@19..20
          LONG@19..20 "1"
    WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
    PIPE@21..22 "|"
    WHITESPACE@22..23 " "
    ERROR@23..38
      IDENT@23..29 "SELECT"
      WHITESPACE@29..30 " "
      IDENT@30..31 "a"
      WHITESPACE@31..32 " "
      IDENT@32..36 "FROM"
      WHITESPACE@36..37 " "
      IDENT@37..38 "t"
  WHITESPACE@38..39 "\n"

error[DS0105]: `SELECT ... FROM` is SQL, not DataScript
  --> query:1:24
  |
1 | from t | where x > 1 | SELECT a FROM t
  |                        ^^^^^^   ---- in DataScript the table comes first
  |                        |
  |                        this is SQL
  |
  = help: start with the table and pipe it into operators: `'orders.csv' | where amount > 10 | project customerId, amount`
